==English==

===Etymology===
{{PIE root|en|h₁es}}
From {{inh|en|enm|potent}}, borrowed from {{der|en|la|potens|potens, potentis|t=powerful, strong, potent}}, present participle of {{m|la|posse|t=to be able}}, from {{m|la|potis|t=able, powerful, originally a lord, master}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpəʊt(ə)nt/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{enPR|pōtʹnt}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpoʊt(ə)nt/|[ˈpʰoʊ̯ʔn̩t]|[-n̩ʔ]}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊtənt}}
* {{hyphenation|en|po|tent}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Possessing [[strength]].
#: {{ux|en|a '''potent''' argument}}
#* {{RQ:Vance Nobody|I}}
#*: Little disappointed, then, she turned attention to "Chat of the Social World," gossip which exercised '''potent''' fascination upon the girl's intelligence.
# Powerfully [[effective]].
#: {{ux|en|a '''potent''' medicine}}
# Having a [[sharp]] or [[offensive]] taste.
# {{lb|en|of a male}} Able to [[procreate]].
# [[very|Very]] [[powerful]] or [[effective]].
#* {{rfdatek|en|William Shakespeare}}
#*: harsh and '''potent''' injuries
#* {{rfdatek|en|John Milton}}
#*: Moses once more his '''potent''' rod extends.

